IS 327 Final Project Report
Predicting Heart Disease Diagnosis with Machine Learning Models
Arianna Caveney
Course: IS327 – Concepts of Machine Learning
Date: May 11, 2025

1. Introduction
The objective of this project is to apply machine learning models to determine if a patient does or does not have heart disease based on 13 features such as chest pain type and specific test results. This project illustrates a complete picture of the machine learning pipeline from the processing of the data itself to tuning selected models based on performance. 

2. Dataset Description
•	Source: UCI Machine Learning Repository – Heart Disease dataset (Cleveland subset)
•	Type: Binary classification task
•	Instances:
o	Originally 303 patient records
o	Reduced to 297 after dropping rows with missing values
•	Features:

13 clinical variables per patient, including:
o	age: age in years
o	sex: 0 = female, 1 = male
o	cp: chest pain type (4 categories)
o	trestbps: resting blood pressure
o	chol: serum cholesterol
o	fbs: fasting blood sugar > 120 mg/dl
o	restecg: resting ECG results
o	thalach: maximum heart rate achieved
o	exang: exercise-induced angina
o	oldpeak: ST depression from exercise
o	slope: slope of the ST segment
o	ca: number of major vessels visualized by fluoroscopy
o	thal: thalassemia test result (normal, fixed defect, reversible defect)

•	Target Variable:
o	target: originally 0–4 indicating severity
o	Binarized to:
	0 = no heart disease
	1 = presence of heart disease (≥50% arterial narrowing)

•	Missing Values:
o	Present in ca and thal (represented as ?)
o	Handled by converting to NaN and dropping affected rows (6 total)

•	Class Balance:
o	168 patients with no heart disease
o	129 patients with heart disease
o	Nearly balanced for a medical classification task

•	Data Handling:
o	Loaded using pandas
o	Cleaned and converted to numeric
o	Scaled using StandardScaler to prepare for modeling

3. Data Preprocessing
•	Verified class distribution: 168 no heart disease, 129 with heart disease
•	Features scaled using StandardScaler to zero mean and unit variance
•	Target variable binarized: 0 = no disease, 1 = disease present
•	Missing values in ca and thal: converted to NaN and dropped 6 affected rows
•	No categorical encoding needed: all features were already numeric or encoded as integers


4. Feature Engineering
I decided against feature engineering since the AUC-ROC was already around 0.96. I think if it did have a positive impact on the model, it would only be marginal, so I did not include it. Some potential routes could have been:
•	Cholesterol based on age – variable that normalizes cholesterol based on expected age-related increase
•	Chest pain type – an abnormal vs. normal chest pain type Boolean, rather than the three or so different types
•	Single risk load score – a value that combines fbs, exang, and/or ca to create the sum of all risk factor scores


5. Model Development
•	In order to move forward with a model with the best performance possible, I implemented three classification models: Logistic Regression, Support Vector Machine (SVM), and Random Forest Classifier
•	Random Forest performed the best, so it was tuned using 5-fold cross-validation
o	Model Tuning via GridSearchCV for Hyperparameter tuning
o	Bias-variance analysis using learning_curve()


6. Underfitting and Overfitting
•	Learning curves were plotted for the tuned Random Forest; 5-fold cross-validation used
•	The models were initially severely overfitted with the training accuracy at 1.0, but this was corrected after tuning
•	Training accuracy dropped and the validation curve was more stable, indicating an improvement in generalization of the model
•	The previous models also showed early signs of this: logistic regression slightly underfit the data, while SVM showed small indications of overfitting


7. Model Evaluation
•	Evaluation metrics: Accuracy, Precision, Recall, F1 Score, and AUC-ROC
•	Final test performance per model (approximations):

o	Logistic regression: 
	Accuracy – 0.83
	Precision – 0.846
	F1 – 0.815
	AUC-ROC – 0.95

o	SVM: 
	Accuracy – 0.85
	Precision – 0.88
	F1 – 0.83
	AUC-ROC – 0.954

o	Random Forest: Untuned
	Accuracy – 0.867
	Precision – 0.885
	F1 – 0.85
	AUC-ROC – 0.94

o	Random Forest: Tuned
	Accuracy – 0.85
	Precision – 0.88
	F1 – 0.83
	AUC-ROC – 0.951

•	The confusion matrices showed that the tuned model had relatively few false negatives with a good balance between the true positives and negatives
o	This indicates the model was effective in correctly identifying those with heart disease while missing minimal cases, which would be life-threatening in a clinical setting


8. Conclusion
After testing all of the models, and finding SVM as not learning as well as necessary as data set size increases initially in a separate test, 
Random Forest was the model that was best able to predict heart disease based on these crucial metrics. This is a significant finding in the use of machine 
learning in predictive models in medicine. Using proper processing and tuning models manually play major roles in the efficiency, effectiveness, and 
optimization of features and results. Future direction could include larger studies with more data and features, or even the use of a similar model on other
types of medical data. 


